// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row fatslice_field_match_binder_invalid_mlir (tier 3).
// A FatSlice-repr struct field — `&[T]` or `str`, a {data, len} pair — reached
// through a BY-VALUE binder of a MATCH struct pattern and then PROJECTED emits
// invalid MLIR and the compile dies (rc 1, no source-located diagnostic):
//   error: 'llvm.getelementptr' op operand #0 must be LLVM pointer type ...
//          but got '!llvm.struct<(ptr, i64)>'
//   mlir_gen: module verification failed
// The projection GEPs the field's 16-byte VALUE as if it were the address of
// one. Legal Rust; legal Logos everywhere else.
//
// ⚠ THE CLASS, ENUMERATED BY THE PROPERTY 2026-09-08 on build 2c5a33ef99e94fc9
// 43: 21 shapes compiled, and each that compiled was also linked and RUN. Every
// failing shape prints the SAME sentence, so this is ONE lowering site. The
// property is not "a binder" and not "a struct pattern" — it is A FATSLICE
// PROJECTION WHOSE OPERAND IS A BY-VALUE PATTERN BINDER.
//   FAIL (8), all rc 1, all the same message:
//     * `match h { H { s, n } => s[2] }` — this program;
//     * the same arm with `s.len()` in place of `s[2]`;
//     * `if let H { s, n } = h`;  `while let H { s, n } = h`;
//     * `match &h { H { s, n } => ... }`;
//     * a GUARDED arm, `H { s, n } if n == 7i64 => s[2]`;
//     * the same field on a GENERIC struct `W<T>` instantiated at `&[i64]`;
//     * `str` in the field's place, PROVIDED the binder is projected (`s.len()`).
//   OK (13), each compiled AND RUN rc 0:
//     * THE SAME MATCH WITH THE BINDER NEVER PROJECTED — `H { s, n } => n` —
//       for the `&[i64]` field and for the `str` field. Binding is not the
//       defect; projecting through the binder is.
//     * a `ref` binder — `H { s: ref q, n } => q[2]`. Its operand is an address.
//     * the binder REBOUND before use: `let t: &[i64] = s; t[2]`;
//     * the binder passed to a fn, `third(s)`, and stored into another struct,
//       `G { s: s }`, then projected there;
//     * a TUPLE pattern binder, `match t { (s, n) => s[2] }` (and `s.len()`);
//     * an ENUM PAYLOAD binder, `match e { E::S(s) => s[2] }` — other pattern
//       kinds are clean, it is the struct pattern;
//     * the NESTED case `match o { Out { i, n } => i.s[2] }`: the outer binder
//       is a thin struct, so the projection happens off a place;
//     * the same field bound by a `let` struct pattern, `let H { s, n } = h;`;
//     * a THIN `&i64` field, and a FatDyn `&dyn Tr` field with a method called
//       through the binder, at the same door — so it is the FatSlice repr and
//       not fatness as such;
//     * field projection `h.s[1]` with no pattern anywhere.
//
// ⚠ CORRECTS THE ROW AS MINTED EARLIER THE SAME DAY (commit db2fa5f96). Both of
// its claims are measured FALSE by this enumeration: "BOUND by a name ... emits
// invalid MLIR" — a bound-but-never-projected binder is clean — and "`str` in
// the field's place fails identically, so both FatSlice spellings" — `str`
// fails only when projected, exactly like `&[i64]`, so the spellings agree but
// not for the recorded reason. The program is UNCHANGED; only the reading is.
//
// Provenance: recorded 2026-08-18 in memory bug_zone_mut_reborrow_thin as
// "FatSlice field x struct pattern (compile error)", never rowed; the note did
// not say which pattern door, and the `let` door is the one that works.
package fatslice_field_match_binder_invalid_mlir;
struct H { s: &[i64], n: i64 }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let a: [i64; 3] = [1i64, 2i64, 3i64];
    let h: H = H { s: &a, n: 7i64 };
    match h {
        H { s, n } => {
            if s[2] != 3i64 { return 10i32; }
            if n != 7i64 { return 11i32; }
        }
    }
    return 0i32;
}
